    {
      "name": "Slide",
      "title": "Spacing Principles",
      "subtitle": null,
      "x": 2020,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 1920,
      "h": 1080,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 3,
          "text": "Spacing in SOLAR defines rhythm, separation, and containment. It ensures clarity and hierarchy by systemizing how elements relate to one another. Spacing is controlled through standardized tokens — not manual margin adjustments."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 4,
          "text": "Spacing Creates Relationships"
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 4,
          "text": "Consistent Rhythm Over Precision"
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 4,
          "text": "Interaction-Aware Spacing"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Spacing communicates what belongs together and what does not. Smaller spacing signals grouping and related content, while larger spacing creates separation and hierarchy. Spacing should make structure obvious without relying on borders or color.",
          "style": "Body/Body M/Regular"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Spacing follows a defined scale to create predictable visual rhythm. Values are chosen from tokens, not tuned per layout. Consistent rhythm improves scanability, reduces cognitive load, and keeps layouts cohesive across screens and platforms.",
          "style": "Body/Body M/Regular"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Spacing must account for interaction, not just layout. Touch targets, focus outlines, and interactive states require sufficient space to remain usable and accessible. If spacing breaks interaction or focus behavior, it is a system issue—not a layout exception.",
          "style": "Body/Body M/Regular"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Slide",
      "title": "Spacing Hierarchy",
      "subtitle": null,
      "x": 4040,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 1920,
      "h": 1080,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 3,
          "text": "Spacing in SOLAR is structured in layers. Each layer defines a different role — from page-level boundaries to element-level rhythm. Clear separation of these roles ensures consistency and prevents misuse of tokens."
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 5,
          "text": "Spatial Layers"
        },
        {
          "t": "code",
          "text": "Viewport\n   ↓\nGrid Margin\n   ↓\nInset (inside containers)\n   ↓\nStack (between siblings)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Slide",
      "title": "Sizing Scale",
      "subtitle": null,
      "x": 6060,
      "y": 0,
      "w": 1920,
      "h": 1080,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 3,
          "text": "The sizing scale defines the foundational measurement system for spacing and sizing in SOLAR. It is based on a 4px modular scale, creating a consistent spatial rhythm across layouts, components, and typography."
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "The scale is platform-agnostic, allowing products to implement the values in the units appropriate for their platform (px, rem, dp, pt, etc.). This ensures consistent proportions across web, mobile, and other environments.",
          "style": "Body/Body M/Regular"
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 5,
          "text": "Why SOLAR Uses a Modular Scale"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Using a modular scale creates a consistent spatial rhythm across the interface.\n\nEnsures proportional spacing across layouts\nKeeps typography, spacing, and layout aligned\nImproves visual consistency across products\nEnables predictable behavior across platforms",
          "style": "Body/Body M/Regular"
        },
        {
          "t": "h",
          "level": 5,
          "text": "Primitive Source"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Defined in: Primitives → spatial → scale. All semantic spacing tokens reference this scale.\n\nExamples from the scale:\nscale-1 (4px)\nscale-2 (8px)\nscale-3 (12px)\nscale-4 (16px)",
          "style": "Body/Body M/Regular"
        },
        {
          "t": "image",
          "name": "Screenshot 2026-03-13 at 15.35.42 1"
        }
      ]
    },
